Certify that a target VLM `L` correctly determines whether an ego vehicle needs to brake, given a rendered image of a rainy driving scene. The symbolic scene graph `G_sym` is small and hand-crafted. It has nodes: ego vehicle node `ν_ego`, road barrier node `ν_barrier`, empty path node `ν_empty`, and rain weather node `ν_rain`. Edges: `ν_ego`–`ν_barrier` with attribute "ahead", `ν_ego`–`ν_empty` with attribute "ahead", `ν_ego`–`ν_rain` with attribute "weather".

Each scenario is sampled by drawing `ν_obs` from `{ν_barrier, ν_empty}` uniformly, then retrieving the edge between `ν_ego` and `ν_obs`. The symbolic subgraph `g_sym` is assembled from `ν_ego`, `ν_obs`, `ν_rain`, and the retrieved edges. A simulator command string is formed from the template "Setup {ν_ego} with {ν_obs} ahead in {ν_rain} weather" and passed to an external tool `Scenic` via `tool-call` to render a concrete driving scene image `img`. The VLM is queried with `img` and the text question "Is it necessary for the vehicle to stop here?". The ground truth `gt` is the string "yes" if `ν_obs = ν_barrier` and "no" otherwise; this must be computed explicitly as a program variable before the judge is called. An external judge `J_VLM_QA` takes the VLM response `r` and the ground truth string `gt` and returns 1 if they match. Use 200 samples at 95% confidence with the Clopper-Pearson certifier.